<h2>Heaps</h2>
<p>
    Heaps are a type of complete binary tree.
    <br>
    <br>
    A binary tree is a common type of data structure.
    Binary trees are made up of elements called nodes,
    with the first node often called the root node.
    Each node can have up to two child nodes, 
    which can then go on to have up to two child nodes of their own.
    <br>
    A complete binary tree is a binary tree that has every level, 
    except possibly the last, completely filled. Additionally, 
    all nodes in the last level are as far left as possible.
    <br>
    <br>
    There are two types of heaps: Maximum and Minimum Heaps.
</p>

<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            In Maximum Heaps, each node is greater or equal to both of its children nodes.
            This results in the largest element in the heap being the root node, 
            elements getting smaller the farther down the tree they are,
            and the smallest elements being at the bottom of the heap.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            In Minimum Heaps, each node is less than or equal to both of its children nodes.
            This results in the smallest element in the heap being the root node,
            elements getting larger the farther down the tree they are,
            and the largest elements being at the bottom of the heap.
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    Arrays are often used to represent heaps. 
    When doing so, we make a couple of assumptions: 
</p>

<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            To find a node's children in an array we multiply the node by two and then add one or two
            to find the left and right child nodes respectively.
            So the left child node is at Root*2 + 1,
            and the right child node is at Root*2 + 2.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            All of the nodes with children are located in the first half of the array,
            and all of the nodes in the bottom level of the heap are in the second half of the array.
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
    
<p>
    To turn an array into a heap we start at the last parent node and its child nodes.
    Then we check if the parent is less than either of its children (if we are making a max heap),
    or greater than either of its children (if we are making a min heap).
    If it is, we swap the parent node with the corresponding child node.
    <br>
    <br>
    We move backward through the parent nodes, repeating this process for each one.
    Any time we swap a parent and either of its child nodes,
    we jump to that child node and repeat the process there before continuing to the next parent node.
    Once this process is complete, we have a heap!
</p>

<h2>Max Heap Sort</h2>
<p>
    Max Heap Sort begins by turning the array into a max heap. 
    This process is described above.
    <br>
    <br>
    After turning the array into a max heap, the largest element in the array is now
    at the beginning of the heap. 
    Then we switch the first element of the heap with the last element of the array.
    This puts the largest element of the array in its correct place at the end of the array.
    <br>
    <br>
    This also places the last element in the heap, 
    which will be one of the smallest elements in the array,
    at the beginning of the heap. This means that the array is no longer a heap.
    To turn it into a heap again we do the same process as described in the Heaps section above:
    we switch the root with the larger child node, then do the same thing with that child node.
    This turns the array into a max heap again.
    <br>
    <br>
    We repeat this entire process until the heap is just one element, 
    the smallest element in the array.
    At this point the array is sorted.
</p>

<h2>Min Heap Sort</h2>
<p>
    Min Heap Sort uses the same process described in Max Heap Sort,
    but using min heaps instead of max heaps.
    However, this results in the array now being backwards, 
    starting with the largest element and ending with the smallest.
    To finish the sort, we reverse the array to put it in order.
    This makes it slower than Max Heap Sort.
</p>

<h2>Reverse Min Heap Sort</h2>
<p>
    Reverse Min Heap Sort is the same of Min Heap Sort,
    but it builds the heap so that it starts at the end of the array
    and ends at the start of the array.
    This builds the array starting from smallest to largest, 
    avoiding the need to reverse the array like in Min Heap Sort.
</p>

<h2>Time Complexity of Heap Sort</h2>
<p>
    The process of building the heap takes <b>logn</b> time.
    Then, the process of extracting the root element from the heap takes <b>n</b> time.
    This gives Heap Sort and its variations a Time Complexity of <b>nlogn</b>.
    <br>
    <br>
    Although Min Heap Sort has to spend extra time reversing the array,
    it still has a Time Complexity of <b>nlogn</b>.
</p>

